WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:03.600
Window functions, or sometimes we call them analytical functions.

00:03.600 --> 00:05.880
They are very important functions in SQL.

00:06.080 --> 00:09.320
Everyone must know them, especially if you are doing data analysis.

00:09.600 --> 00:14.200
Each time I write SQL script in order to do data analytics, I end up using them.

00:14.320 --> 00:18.120
So as usual, we're going to go and now understand the concept behind them.

00:18.120 --> 00:19.760
And then we're going to start practicing.

00:19.840 --> 00:20.560
So let's go.

00:24.520 --> 00:25.080
Okay guys.

00:25.080 --> 00:26.920
So now let's start with the first question.

00:26.920 --> 00:29.080
What are SQL window functions.

00:29.320 --> 00:35.640
They are functions that allows you to do calculations like aggregations, but on top of subset of data

00:35.840 --> 00:38.800
without losing the level of details of the rows.

00:38.840 --> 00:41.160
So it is something very similar to the groupby.

00:41.200 --> 00:42.640
But here we have special case.

00:42.960 --> 00:44.920
You don't lose the level of details.

00:45.000 --> 00:48.680
So now in order to understand the definition let's have a very simple example.

00:48.720 --> 00:48.880
Okay.

00:48.920 --> 00:52.560
So now let's understand how SQL works with the group by clause.

00:52.600 --> 00:54.680
Let's say that we have the very simple example.

00:54.680 --> 00:59.160
We have four orders two orders for the cabs and two orders for the gloves.

00:59.320 --> 01:03.520
And let's say that I would like to see the total sales for each products.

01:03.520 --> 01:08.800
So now if we decided to use the groupby, what SQL going to do, going to take the first two orders

01:08.800 --> 01:11.120
for the caps and put it in one row.

01:11.120 --> 01:16.120
So in the output, we're going to have only one row for the caps with the total sales of 40.

01:16.160 --> 01:18.080
And the same thing can happen for the gloves.

01:18.080 --> 01:20.840
So it's going to take the two rows of the gloves from the input.

01:20.840 --> 01:23.840
And in the output we're going to have only one row for the gloves.

01:23.840 --> 01:29.480
So that means the number of rows is going to be depending on the number of products we have on our data.

01:29.480 --> 01:30.400
We have two products.

01:30.400 --> 01:31.840
We get two rows.

01:31.880 --> 01:37.200
So that means SQL is really like smashing or squeezing the results in the output.

01:37.200 --> 01:39.600
And this is exactly what the groupby does to our data.

01:39.600 --> 01:44.240
It aggregates the rows, aggregate the data into different level of details.

01:44.240 --> 01:46.720
So now on the left side we see four rows.

01:46.720 --> 01:49.000
On the right side we have two rows.

01:49.200 --> 01:52.040
And with that we are losing some details in the results.

01:52.040 --> 01:53.760
But still we have solved the tasks.

01:53.760 --> 01:57.080
So now let's see what's going to happen if you use window function in SQL.

01:57.120 --> 01:57.320
Okay.

01:57.360 --> 01:58.610
So now we have the same data.

01:58.610 --> 02:02.570
And as with the same task, we have to find the total sales for each product.

02:02.730 --> 02:06.250
Now if we use window function SQL going to do the following.

02:06.490 --> 02:10.290
It's going to go and execute each rows individually from each others.

02:10.530 --> 02:11.490
So what can happen.

02:11.490 --> 02:14.090
It starts with the first row the order ID one.

02:14.450 --> 02:15.090
In the output.

02:15.090 --> 02:15.850
We're going to get as well.

02:15.850 --> 02:18.730
The same stuff the order ID one, the same row.

02:18.930 --> 02:21.970
But we will get the total sales for the caps.

02:21.970 --> 02:24.130
So here the total sales is going to be ten plus 30.

02:24.170 --> 02:25.410
We will get 40.

02:25.450 --> 02:28.850
Then it's going to jump to the second row and it's going to process it as well.

02:28.850 --> 02:32.410
So in the output we will get the order ID two, the product caps.

02:32.410 --> 02:36.370
And as well we have the same aggregation since we are talking about the same product.

02:36.370 --> 02:37.770
So we will get 40.

02:37.810 --> 02:39.250
Then it's going to go to the third order.

02:39.250 --> 02:40.330
And here we have the gloves.

02:40.330 --> 02:45.250
So in the output again we have the order ID three, the product gloves and the total sales.

02:45.250 --> 02:47.530
This time going to be five plus 20.

02:47.570 --> 02:48.930
So we'll get 25.

02:49.090 --> 02:52.130
Then it goes to the last row to the order ID number four.

02:52.330 --> 02:55.650
In the output we're going to get four gloves and as well 25.

02:55.650 --> 03:01.290
So now we can notice that if we use the window function, you will not lose the level of details of

03:01.290 --> 03:01.930
your data.

03:02.090 --> 03:05.090
So we are doing something called row level calculations.

03:05.210 --> 03:09.810
So if in input data we have four orders in the output we're going to get four orders.

03:09.810 --> 03:12.810
And as well we will get our aggregations correctly.

03:12.810 --> 03:17.970
So now if you compare both of the methods side by side we can see that we are solving the same task.

03:17.970 --> 03:20.730
So we are finding the total sales for each products.

03:20.930 --> 03:28.130
But with the groupby we are smashing, squeezing the results from four orders into two rows, one row

03:28.130 --> 03:29.290
for each order.

03:29.290 --> 03:33.610
So that means with a groupby the granularity is changing right in the input.

03:33.610 --> 03:39.210
The order id is controlling the level of details, but in the output of the groupby, the product is

03:39.210 --> 03:40.570
controlling the level of detail.

03:40.570 --> 03:42.170
So we have different granularity.

03:42.170 --> 03:47.090
But in the other hand, in the window functions we are still able to do aggregations, but we are not

03:47.090 --> 03:48.850
losing the level of details.

03:48.850 --> 03:53.410
So the granularity of the input can be the same like the output in the results.

03:53.410 --> 03:57.340
So this is exactly the main difference between the groupie and the window function.

03:57.660 --> 04:01.340
If you want just to do simple aggregations, then go with the groupie.

04:01.620 --> 04:07.100
But if you care about the level of details and you need to add more details to your results, then you

04:07.100 --> 04:11.860
can go with the window function where you can do aggregations plus having more details.

04:11.900 --> 04:16.500
And now if you go and compare the functions between the window and the groupby, we can find that both

04:16.500 --> 04:19.260
of them has exactly the same functions for the aggregations.

04:19.260 --> 04:21.940
So we have the count, sum, average, min, max.

04:21.940 --> 04:26.980
And here comes another difference between the window and the group by the groupby has only the aggregate

04:26.980 --> 04:27.540
functions.

04:27.540 --> 04:28.380
So that's it.

04:28.380 --> 04:33.220
But the window functions we have way more functions to use for analytics.

04:33.220 --> 04:35.500
So for example we have the ranking functions.

04:35.500 --> 04:39.980
And we have here another group of functions for the value or we call it analytical functions.

04:39.980 --> 04:42.940
So that means in the SQL window we have a lot of functions.

04:42.940 --> 04:47.700
We can cover a lot of analytical use cases and advanced complex stuff.

04:47.780 --> 04:52.860
But with a groupby we have only the aggregate functions only for simple use cases.

04:52.860 --> 04:56.420
So this is another difference between the groupby and the window groupby.

04:56.460 --> 05:00.900
Use it if you have a simple analysis simple aggregations window functions.

05:00.900 --> 05:05.580
We're going to use it for more advanced data analysis where we're going to cover a lot of use cases.

05:05.620 --> 05:06.180
All right guys.

05:06.180 --> 05:08.980
So now we're going to have a few tasks in order to understand one thing.

05:09.260 --> 05:14.860
Why do we need SQL window functions and why in some scenarios groupby is not enough.

05:14.860 --> 05:17.220
And we have to use SQL window functions.

05:17.220 --> 05:17.820
So let's go.

05:17.860 --> 05:18.180
All right.

05:18.180 --> 05:19.860
So let's start with a very simple task.

05:19.860 --> 05:23.740
It's going to say find the total sales across all orders.

05:23.900 --> 05:26.340
So we need one value the total sales.

05:26.380 --> 05:27.820
Let's see how we can do that.

05:27.940 --> 05:30.580
First make sure that you are using the database.

05:30.580 --> 05:36.340
So use sales database in case you have closed the clients so that you don't get any errors.

05:36.340 --> 05:38.180
So now we're going to start with the first thing.

05:38.180 --> 05:40.100
We're going to go and select the sales.

05:40.100 --> 05:43.500
You're going to find it in the table sales orders.

05:43.700 --> 05:45.060
So now you just query the data.

05:45.060 --> 05:48.060
And as you can see we have ten orders with ten sales.

05:48.060 --> 05:49.940
We didn't aggregate anything yet.

05:49.940 --> 05:51.500
So we have the raw data now.

05:51.500 --> 05:54.940
So now in order to solve the task, we can use the function sum.

05:55.180 --> 05:58.060
So sum of sales and we're going to give it a new name.

05:58.100 --> 05:59.260
Total sales.

05:59.260 --> 06:03.380
We don't have to use any groupby because we don't have to group up anything.

06:03.380 --> 06:04.340
So that's it.

06:04.340 --> 06:05.820
Let's go and execute that.

06:05.820 --> 06:09.180
And as you can see SQL can return one value 380.

06:09.460 --> 06:12.140
This is the total sales that we have inside our data.

06:12.380 --> 06:15.380
And this is the highest level of aggregations.

06:15.380 --> 06:16.980
So with that we have solved the task.

06:16.980 --> 06:20.020
We have the total sales across all orders.

06:20.020 --> 06:21.660
We don't have to group up anything.

06:21.660 --> 06:23.100
Let's move to the next example.

06:23.140 --> 06:24.780
Let's see that in the next task.

06:24.780 --> 06:29.300
This time we want to find the total sales but for each products.

06:29.460 --> 06:34.020
So not for the orders for each product we want to find the total sales.

06:34.020 --> 06:36.220
So this time we don't need only one value.

06:36.260 --> 06:38.700
We need one value for each product.

06:38.700 --> 06:39.900
In order to do that.

06:39.940 --> 06:42.820
Now we're going to go and use the groupby function.

06:42.820 --> 06:49.100
And we're going to group by the product ID and group up need as well the dimension in the selection.

06:49.100 --> 06:51.070
So I'm going to do it like this.

06:51.070 --> 06:51.830
So that's it.

06:51.830 --> 06:53.470
Let's go and execute the query.

06:53.510 --> 06:55.470
Now as you can see in the results we don't have one value.

06:55.470 --> 06:57.270
We don't have the highest aggregations.

06:57.550 --> 07:01.470
This time we are drilling down to the next level of details.

07:01.470 --> 07:06.630
So the level of details here is the product ID we have one row for each product.

07:06.630 --> 07:09.070
So for the first product we have 140.

07:09.150 --> 07:11.270
The next one 105 and so on.

07:11.270 --> 07:15.830
So as you can see we are now splitting the data at the level of product ID.

07:15.830 --> 07:17.790
And we went from ten orders.

07:17.830 --> 07:19.870
Now in the results we have four orders.

07:19.870 --> 07:22.390
And that's because we have four products.

07:22.390 --> 07:28.270
So the number of rows at the output going to be defined by the dimension the product ID.

07:28.270 --> 07:29.830
And with that we have solved the task.

07:29.830 --> 07:32.070
We have the total sales for each product.

07:32.070 --> 07:32.670
All right guys.

07:32.670 --> 07:34.710
So let's keep progressing in our examples.

07:34.750 --> 07:38.870
Now the next one is going to be a little bit advanced where we have the same aggregation.

07:38.910 --> 07:41.270
Find the total sales for each product.

07:41.430 --> 07:46.030
Additionally provide details such order ID and the order date.

07:46.030 --> 07:50.670
So as you can see, we have already solved the first part, we are finding the total sales for each

07:50.670 --> 07:51.310
product.

07:51.350 --> 07:55.430
Now we just have to add some additional information like the order ID and the order date.

07:55.550 --> 07:58.190
So let's go over here and just add it in our select.

07:58.190 --> 08:01.550
So order ID and let's have the order dates.

08:01.630 --> 08:03.630
So let's go and execute that.

08:03.630 --> 08:05.230
Just going to make it a little bit bigger.

08:05.350 --> 08:05.910
So let's go.

08:05.910 --> 08:11.110
But now as you can see SQL will not be happy and throw an error and says the stuff that you are adding

08:11.150 --> 08:14.430
to your selects are not included in the group by.

08:14.470 --> 08:18.790
So as you can see in the group by, we have only one dimension or one field called the product ID,

08:18.950 --> 08:24.190
but in our selection we have three dimensions the order ID, the order date, and the product ID.

08:24.350 --> 08:29.190
So there is no matching between the select and group by and SQL will not allow it.

08:29.190 --> 08:30.510
And now you might say you know what.

08:30.550 --> 08:32.390
Let's add everything to the group by.

08:32.590 --> 08:34.190
So with that we're going to get our aggregation.

08:34.190 --> 08:35.870
And as well we're going to get our details.

08:36.070 --> 08:37.030
So let's try that.

08:37.030 --> 08:38.670
I'm just going to zoom out a little bit.

08:38.670 --> 08:42.310
And instead of having the product ID let's add everything.

08:42.310 --> 08:45.790
So the order ID order date and the product ID.

08:45.990 --> 08:49.190
So now we have matching and SQL should not throw any error.

08:49.310 --> 08:50.750
Let's go and execute it.

08:50.790 --> 08:52.750
So now let's check whether we have solved the task.

08:52.790 --> 08:54.550
The task has two parts right.

08:54.590 --> 08:57.270
We have to do the aggregations and to provide details.

08:57.350 --> 08:59.430
So as you can see we have solved the second part.

08:59.470 --> 09:02.070
We have the details with order ID and order dates.

09:02.230 --> 09:06.350
But now the first part finding the total sales for each product is destroyed.

09:06.350 --> 09:09.790
Because if you check the results, we have the product ID 101.

09:09.830 --> 09:11.550
It has the total sales of ten.

09:11.950 --> 09:15.710
But in the third order we have it as a 20 for the same product.

09:15.710 --> 09:17.950
So actually the data is not aggregated.

09:17.950 --> 09:20.590
And that's because we are aggregating at different levels.

09:20.590 --> 09:25.950
And we have included way more stuff that we don't need for the aggregations we are aggregating at the

09:25.950 --> 09:27.190
order ID level.

09:27.190 --> 09:30.070
So as you can see now we are hitting the limits of groupby.

09:30.110 --> 09:35.590
We cannot provide aggregations and as well provide additional information from our data.

09:35.590 --> 09:36.470
You have to pick one.

09:36.470 --> 09:40.630
That's why we have to go to the second option where we can use the window functions.

09:40.630 --> 09:41.670
So let's do that.

09:41.670 --> 09:46.040
I'm just going to get rid of the groupby parts and as well all the fields.

09:46.080 --> 09:47.040
Let's back to the roots.

09:47.040 --> 09:48.760
So now we have the sum of sales.

09:48.960 --> 09:51.320
And if I execute this I'm going to get one value.

09:51.320 --> 09:53.400
So we are at the highest level of aggregations.

09:53.640 --> 09:55.560
So now we need to use the window function.

09:55.560 --> 09:57.560
I'm just going to remove the name.

09:57.560 --> 10:03.480
And now we're going to tell SQL this is a window functions using over after the aggregations or the

10:03.480 --> 10:05.000
functions tells SQL.

10:05.040 --> 10:06.880
We are talking about window functions.

10:06.880 --> 10:08.520
So let's just execute it like this.

10:08.520 --> 10:10.680
And with that we got ten rows.

10:10.720 --> 10:13.160
And that's because we have ten orders.

10:13.200 --> 10:15.640
And for each row we have exactly the same value.

10:15.640 --> 10:19.160
So we have the total sales of all orders for each row.

10:19.160 --> 10:24.240
So as you can see SQL understands this is a window function and SQL should not lie.

10:24.280 --> 10:25.800
Group all the data in one row.

10:25.960 --> 10:30.280
It should keep exactly the same rows or same number of rows like the input.

10:30.600 --> 10:32.360
So with that we have the window function.

10:32.360 --> 10:34.680
But we have to split the data by the products.

10:35.120 --> 10:37.440
So now we're going to use the keyword partition by.

10:37.480 --> 10:38.360
It's like the group by.

10:38.360 --> 10:41.560
But another wording products id the same dimension.

10:41.560 --> 10:46.640
So with that we have the total sales by products as a name.

10:46.640 --> 10:48.080
So let's go and execute this.

10:48.080 --> 10:50.960
So now as you can see in the output we still have the same number of rows.

10:50.960 --> 10:52.040
We have ten orders.

10:52.040 --> 10:53.360
We have ten rows.

10:53.520 --> 10:58.160
But the result did change because now we are aggregating the data at the level of product ID.

10:58.360 --> 11:02.600
In order to understand the results, we have to add more information to our selects.

11:02.600 --> 11:04.560
So now let's add the same dimension.

11:04.560 --> 11:08.320
It's going to be the product ID I'm just going to add it at the front over here.

11:08.320 --> 11:09.960
So let's select.

11:09.960 --> 11:12.160
And as you can see now it makes more sense.

11:12.160 --> 11:13.560
We have those products.

11:13.560 --> 11:17.160
And they have always the exact same sales.

11:17.160 --> 11:19.280
And as well for the next product and so on.

11:19.280 --> 11:21.240
And now here comes the magic of the window function.

11:21.240 --> 11:26.240
We can add more informations to our select statement without having any errors.

11:26.240 --> 11:29.040
So now we need additional informations like the order ID.

11:29.200 --> 11:34.720
So we can go over here and say order ID order date, any type of column.

11:34.720 --> 11:37.840
You can add it to your select and let's go and execute.

11:37.840 --> 11:39.600
So as you can see now we got the result.

11:39.600 --> 11:45.330
Even though that those three dimensions in the select are not part of the window aggregation.

11:45.570 --> 11:47.490
So with that we have solved the tasks.

11:47.490 --> 11:48.850
We have additional information.

11:48.850 --> 11:54.730
We have the order ID, the order dates, and as well the first part of the task to find the total sales

11:54.730 --> 11:55.770
for each products.

11:55.770 --> 11:59.370
So each of those values are the total sales for each products.

11:59.570 --> 12:01.570
And with that we have solved the tasks.

12:01.570 --> 12:04.130
And this is exactly why we need window functions.

12:04.130 --> 12:06.730
In real projects things get really complicated.

12:06.730 --> 12:10.570
You are doing different tasks in one query, so you are doing aggregations.

12:10.570 --> 12:12.290
You are doing some other stuff.

12:12.650 --> 12:15.410
So just focusing on the aggregations is not going to be enough.

12:15.450 --> 12:18.730
You have always to add additional informations to your query.

12:18.730 --> 12:21.730
So as you can see we use groupby to do simple analysis.

12:21.730 --> 12:28.210
But as things get complicated in the analytics, we use the window functions in order to show the aggregations

12:28.210 --> 12:30.250
and as well add additional informations.

12:30.250 --> 12:33.250
So as you can see we use groupby to do simple analysis.

12:33.250 --> 12:39.690
But as things get complicated in the analytics, we use the window functions in order to show the aggregations

12:39.690 --> 12:41.690
and as well add additional informations.
